# 4.4 LOGICAL OPERATORS

The logical AND (`&&`) and logical OR (`||`) operators allow you to connect multiple Boolean expressions to create a compound expression.

The logical NOT (`!`) operator reverses the truth of a Boolean expression.

### Logical Operators Table

| Operator | Meaning | Description                                                              |    |                                                                                 |
| -------- | ------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ | -- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| `&&`     | AND     | Both subexpressions must be true for the compound expression to be true. |    |                                                                                 |
| `        |         | `                                                                        | OR | One or both subexpressions must be true for the compound expression to be true. |
| `!`      | NOT     | It negates or reverses the value to its opposite.                        |    |                                                                                 |

### Examples of Logical Expressions

| Expression       | Meaning                                   |         |                                     |
| ---------------- | ----------------------------------------- | ------- | ----------------------------------- |
| `x > y && a < b` | Is x greater than y AND is a less than b? |         |                                     |
| `x == y          |                                           | x == z` | Is x equal to y OR is x equal to z? |
| `!(x > y)`       | Is the expression x > y NOT true?         |         |                                     |

### Practice Lab

The presentation includes a practice lab for logical operators.

### Sample Decision Structures with Logical Operators

**The `&&` operator:**

```csharp
if (temperature < 20 && minutes > 12)
{
    MessageBox.Show("The temperature is in the danger zone.");
}
```

The condition is true only when both expressions are true.

**The `||` operator:**

```csharp
if (temperature < 20 || temperature > 100)
{
    MessageBox.Show("The temperature is in the danger zone.");
}
```

The condition is true when at least one of the expressions is true.

**The `!` operator:**

```csharp
if (!(temperature > 100))
{
    MessageBox.Show("The temperature is below the maximum.");
}
```

The NOT operator reverses the result of the expression `temperature > 100`.

