# 4.9 RADIO BUTTONS AND CHECK BOXES

### Radio Buttons

Radio buttons allow users to select one choice from several possible choices.

When a user clicks on a radio button, the program automatically deselects any other radio button in the same group. This is known as **mutually exclusive selection**: you can select only one option at a time.

### Check Boxes

Check boxes allow users to select multiple options.

---

## RadioButton Control

.NET provides the `RadioButton` control for creating a group of radio buttons.

### Common Properties

**Text:** Holds the text displayed next to the radio button.

**Checked:** A Boolean property that determines whether the control is selected or deselected.

### Checking Whether a RadioButton Is Selected

In code, use a decision structure to determine whether a RadioButton is selected.

Example:

```csharp
if (choiceRadioButton.Checked)
{
    // The radio button is selected.
}
else
{
    // The radio button is not selected.
}
```

You do not need to use the `==` operator, although the following is equivalent:

```csharp
if (choiceRadioButton.Checked == true)
{
    // The radio button is selected.
}
else
{
    // The radio button is not selected.
}
```

---

## CheckBox Control

The .NET Framework provides the `CheckBox` control to give the user an option, such as true/false or yes/no.

### Common Properties

**Text:** Holds the text displayed next to the check box.

**Checked:** A Boolean property that determines whether the control is selected or deselected.

### Checking Whether a CheckBox Is Selected

Example:

```csharp
if (option1CheckBox.Checked)
{
    // The check box is selected.
}
else
{
    // The check box is not selected.
}
```

---

