# 4.11 INTRODUCTION TO LIST BOXES

### What Is a ListBox?

A `ListBox` control displays a list of items and allows the user to select an item from the list.

### Commonly Used Properties

**1. Text**

Contains the text of the currently selected item in the ListBox.

**2. Items**

Contains the items of the ListBox.

**3. Items.Count**

Contains the number of items in the ListBox.

**4. SelectedItem**

Gets or sets the currently selected item in the ListBox. When the user selects an item, that item is stored in this property.

You can use the following to get the selected item from a ListBox:

```csharp
selectedFruit = fruitListBox.SelectedItem.ToString();
```

**5. SelectedIndex**

Gets or sets the zero-based index of the currently selected item in a ListBox.

The first item has index `0`, the second has index `1`, and so on.

---

## Adding Items to a ListBox

The `Items.Add` method allows you to add an item to the ListBox.

### General Syntax

```csharp
ListBox.Items.Add(NewItem);
```

Where:

* `ListBox` is the name of the ListBox control.
* `NewItem` is the value to be added to the ListBox.

### Example

```csharp
daysListBox.Items.Add("Monday");
daysListBox.Items.Add("Tuesday");
daysListBox.Items.Add("Wednesday");
```

This adds three items to the ListBox.

---

## SelectedItem

An exception will occur if you try to get the value of a ListBox's `SelectedItem` property when no item is selected.

### ListBox Indexes

Items in a ListBox have an index starting with `0`.

* The first item has index `0`.
* The nth item has index `n - 1`.
* The `SelectedIndex` property keeps the index.
* If no item is selected, the value of `SelectedIndex` is `-1`.

### Checking Whether an Item Is Selected

The following example uses the `SelectedIndex` property to make sure an item is selected before getting its value from `SelectedItem`.

```csharp
if (fruitListBox.SelectedIndex != -1)
{
    string selectedFruit = fruitListBox.SelectedItem.ToString();
}
```

---

## Difference Between Text and SelectedItem

| Feature     | Text                                     | SelectedItem                            |
| ----------- | ---------------------------------------- | --------------------------------------- |
| Type        | `string`                                 | `object`                                |
| Use case    | Quick access to selected text            | Full access to the selected item        |
| Flexibility | Less flexible                            | More flexible; can be cast to an object |
| Null-safe?  | Might return `""` if nothing is selected | Might be `null` if nothing is selected  |
| Best for    | Simple cases with text-only lists        | Complex or data-bound lists             |

### Another Comparison

| Property       | Type     | Best Use For                  | Behind the Scenes                            |
| -------------- | -------- | ----------------------------- | -------------------------------------------- |
| `SelectedItem` | `object` | When working with real data   | Can be an object such as Student, Book, etc. |
| `Text`         | `string` | When you only care about text | Gives the name shown in the ListBox          |

---

## Removing Items from a ListBox: Items.Remove

The `Items.Remove` method removes a specific item from a ListBox.

### General Syntax

```csharp
ListBox.Items.Remove(Item);
```

Where:

* `ListBox` is the name of the ListBox control.
* `Item` is the item to be removed from the ListBox.

### Example

```csharp
daysListBox.Items.Remove("Tuesday");
```

This removes the item `"Tuesday"` from the ListBox.

---

## Removing Items from a ListBox: Items.RemoveAt

The `Items.RemoveAt` method removes an item at a specific index from a ListBox.

### General Syntax

```csharp
ListBox.Items.RemoveAt(Index);
```

Where:

* `ListBox` is the name of the ListBox control.
* `Index` is the index of the item to be removed from the ListBox.

### Example

```csharp
daysListBox.Items.RemoveAt(2);
```

This removes the item at index `2`, which is the third item in the list.

**Important note:** The specified index must be valid. It cannot be less than `0` or greater than or equal to the number of items in the ListBox. If the index is invalid, the `RemoveAt` method will throw an exception.

---

## Clearing All Items from a ListBox

The `Items.Clear` method erases all the items in the ListBox.

### Example

```csharp
daysListBox.Items.Clear();
```

This removes every item from the ListBox.

